# Command-line interface

@lede
Two binaries: `brief` (the compiler) and `brief-web` (the static-site
generator). Everything else lives behind a subcommand.
@end

## `brief`

The compiler. Subcommands: `compile`, `convert`, `explain`.

### `brief compile`

Compile a `.brf` file to one of three targets.

```bash
brief compile <file.brf> --target=<html|llm|json> [options]
```

Options:

@dl
--target=html | llm | json
: The output format. Default is `html`, override in `brief.toml` with
  `compile.default_target`.
-w, --write
: Write the output to a file next to the source. `.brf` becomes `.html`,
  `.txt`, or `.json` depending on the target.
-o, --output PATH
: Write to a specific path. Mutually exclusive with `-w`.
--report-tokens
: Only valid with `--target=llm`. Prints a stderr line with the cl100k
  character/token estimate of the output.
--strip-emphasis
: LLM target only. Drop emphasis markers entirely; text content survives.
--keep-asset-urls
: LLM target only. Preserve image URLs in `@image` rendering. Default is
  to drop them (the alt text alone reaches the LLM).
--keep-table-rule
: LLM target only. Emit a `|---|` line under table headers. Default
  omits it to save tokens.
--keep-metadata
: LLM target only. Emit frontmatter as-is rather than stripping it.
@end

Exit codes:

- `0` — success.
- `1` — one or more compile errors. Diagnostics go to `stderr`.

### `brief convert`

Convert a Markdown document to Brief.

```bash
brief convert notes.md                      # writes notes.brf
brief convert notes.md -o renamed.brf       # rename single output
brief convert notes.md --stdout             # pipe to stdout
brief convert *.md                          # batch
```

The converter is *lossy by design*. Constructs without a clean Brief
equivalent are flagged as design holes — written to stderr and inserted
into the output as `// TODO[B-hole:...]` comments. Inline HTML, raw HTML
blocks, and YAML frontmatter are the most common holes.

Per-file failures in batch mode do not abort the run; each file is
converted independently.

### `brief explain`

Print the long-form explanation of an error code.

```bash
brief explain B0204
```

Output is the rule, why it exists, a worked example of the error, and a
worked example of the fix. See @ref[reference/error-codes.brf](Error code registry)
for the full list.

## `brief-web`

The static-site generator. Subcommands: `init`, `build`, `serve`.

### `brief-web init`

Scaffold a new project.

```bash
brief-web init my-site            # single-page mode (just index.brf)
brief-web init my-site --multi    # multi-page mode (with SUMMARY.brf)
brief-web init my-site --force    # overwrite existing files
```

### `brief-web build`

One-shot build. Reads `book.toml`, walks `src/`, writes `dist/`.

```bash
brief-web build                # build the project in the current directory
brief-web build path/to/site   # build a specific project
```

The previous `dist/` contents are cleared before each build.

### `brief-web serve`

Build, then run a development HTTP server with file watching.

```bash
brief-web serve                              # default: 127.0.0.1:3000
brief-web serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080
brief-web serve --no-watch                   # one-shot serve, no live reload
```

A tiny client is injected into every served page. It listens to a
Server-Sent-Events stream at `/__brief_web_events` and reloads the page
when the build finishes. No WebSockets, no polling.

## Common patterns

```bash
# Render and budget a prompt.
brief compile prompt.brf --target=llm --report-tokens > prompt.txt

# Round-trip a Markdown document.
brief convert notes.md && brief compile notes.brf --target=html -w

# Live-edit a docs site.
brief-web serve docs/ &
$EDITOR docs/src/some-page.brf
```
